<section class="boundaries paper-fold" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
  <div class="boundaries__intro">
    <h2 id="boundaries-title">Here is what the post office can see.</h2>
    <p>
      The design limits who can read a letter and what strangers learn about
      each other. It does not pretend the post office is invisible.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="boundaries__facts">
    <div>
      <h3>Your correspondence is protected at rest.</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Correspondence is application-encrypted in PostgreSQL.</li>
        <li>Only the matched participants can request an ordinary message.</li>
        <li>Aliases are non-searchable and reveal no public profile.</li>
        <li>This static website has no letter API, account state, or analytics.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>The post office still handles delivery.</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Orifude is not end-to-end encrypted.</li>
        <li>The authorized post office can decrypt ordinary messages.</li>
        <li>The service sees operational metadata and participant relationships.</li>
        <li>A running-service compromise is outside storage-encryption protection.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <aside class="safety" aria-labelledby="safety-title">
    <h3 id="safety-title">You can report a harmful letter, burn an exchange, or block its sender.</h3>
    <p>
      Reporting hides the exchange, stores separately encrypted evidence for
      restricted moderation, and permanently blocks future matching. A
      delete-only revocation credential can remove a lost identity; it cannot
      read letters or create a session.
    </p>
  </aside>
</section>
